 <article class="vuln card" id="OBFU-02" data-sev="Low" data-cat="Obfuscation" data-search="obfu-02 ollvm control-flow flattening only: .text entropy is 6.86, so the library is not packed obfuscation per-section entropy across arm64 libtopfollow.so shows .text = 6.8621 over 1,591,568 bytes and whole-file entropy = 6.7518. a packed or compressed .text scores above 7.8; 6.86 is the normal range for aarch64 code. the obfuscation is therefore purely control-flow: the aes wrapper at 0x34424 contains 1,085 instructions with a histogram of mov &amp;nbsp;191, movk &amp;nbsp;142, cmp &amp;nbsp;119, cset &amp;nbsp;46, b.eq &amp;nbsp;28, b.ne &amp;nbsp;23 and a single dispatch loop the signature of ollvm bogus-control-flow plus flattening. .rodata is only 37,547 bytes, so the claimed multi-megabyte white-box table cannot exist at any offset in a 1,805,400-byte file. the highest-entropy 4 kib windows in the whole binary are 0x12000-0x13000 at 7.9954 and 0x13000-0x14000 at 7.9613 and those two windows are precisely the aes s-box and inverse s-box, not a white-box table. .eh_frame yields 1,090 well-formed fdes, which a packer would destroy. the binary looks formidable 1.8 mb, a single exported symbol, 22 runtime-registered natives, flattened control flow but none of that is packing, and the absence of packing is what made the whole analysis possible statically. every secret was recoverable without executing the app: 943 position-independent thunks resolved to 760 .rodata references, and a single-byte xor sweep over keys 1-127 recovered the backend url, the instagram endpoints, the certificate pin and the anti-analysis keyword tables. assessing this app's real strength requires separating obfuscation from protection: the former is heavy, the latter is thin. work/out/30_aes_forensics_arm64.txt sections 6-7 (per-section entropy, top entropy windows); instruction histogram of 0x34424 ; .rodata = 37,547 b; 1,090 .eh_frame fdes from work/native_deep.py ; no white-box table at the claimed offsets obfuscation is not a control. spend the budget on server-side verification and hardware-backed attestation; client-side flattening only raises analysis cost, it does not change what an attacker who finishes the analysis can do.">
