++<article class="vuln card" id="CRYP-10" data-sev="High" data-cat="Cryptography" data-search="cryp-10 native aes key is runtime-derived: it is not the caller's key and not a byte window of the file cryptography the unicorn run of the key-expansion routine 0x32158 was given a fips-197 aes-256 test key in a caller buffer laid out three different ways (libc++ short-string, libc++ long-string, and a plain {ptr,len,cap} triple). all three runs were byte-identical: 89,148 instructions, 112 s-box reads, 14 rcon reads, and the same resulting schedule. the expanded schedule therefore does not depend on the key supplied by the caller. a full per-instruction read trace ( work/unicorn_key_trace.py , 9,173 reads) shows the caller key buffer is never read at all ; instead the routine reads the ctx struct byte-by-byte and pulls 52 scattered single bytes from a 549-byte high-entropy table at .rodata 0x13b30 (entropy 7.8099, immediately after rcon, preceded by a 16-byte constant 9a2f5ebc&amp;hellip;c591 at 0x13b1e ). that table is not the s-box, not the inverse, not a permutation and not a gf(2 8 ) log table it is a key-derivation / whitening table that the first content-scan missed because it only looked for the five canonical aes patterns. brute-force over every 16-, 24- and 32-byte window of the 1,805,400-byte file (5.4 million candidates) found no window that expands to the observed rk0 1742e227063cdfce2c2b4cbd71f1297a , and rk0 appears in no file-backed region the routine reads. the key is computed inside the ollvm-flattened prologue at runtime by folding the 0x13b30 table, the 0x13b1e constant and the ctx state together. the cipher's key cannot be extracted by reading the binary, and cannot be influenced by the caller so whatever this aes protects is keyed by a value that exists only during execution. that makes the protection opaque even to the vendor's own java-side code review, and it means the key is either a hardcoded derivation (recoverable by one dynamic trace, and the