++<article class="vuln card" id="CRYP-14" data-sev="Low" data-cat="Cryptography" data-search="cryp-14 opaque 5-byte literal 'f3aes' embedded in the jni metadata cluster of all three abis and referenced by nothing cryptography a 5-byte nul-terminated string f3aes sits at arm64 .rodata+0x14b2f , x86_64 +0x0fdff and x86 +0x092ef . its immediate neighbours are unmistakably jni registration metadata: (lretrofit2/response;)ljava/lang/string; , (zljava/lang/string;)lretrofit2/retrofit; , digest , com/nivaroid/topfollow/helper/t , setup , ()lcom/nivaroid/topfollow/models/instagramaccount; . an exhaustive adrp+add/adr/ldr resolution sweep of the entire arm64 .text found no instruction that computes the address of f3aes , and the string does not appear anywhere in the dex. it also does not decode as base64 under any alignment or prefix. the name reads as a cipher-suite or algorithm identifier, and it is placed among live jni metadata, yet nothing in the binary or the dex consumes it. that is either a dead remnant of a removed or disabled cryptographic feature in which case the shipping binary carries unreferenced crypto naming that will mislead any audit, including this one, and may indicate code paths still reachable by a future or server-flagged build or it is consumed through a computed offset the static sweep cannot model, in which case an analyst cannot rule out an undisclosed algorithm selector. both readings mean the binary contains crypto-related metadata whose liveness cannot be established statically. f3aes present in all three abis at the offsets above; zero resolving instructions across 397,892 arm64 .text instructions; absent from the dex and from work/out/03_all_strings.txt ; adjacent jni metadata strings listed strip unreferenced literals at link time ( -ffunction-sections + --gc-sections ). do not leave dead crypto identifiers in a shipped binary; they cannot be distinguished from live ones by an auditor.">
